@meta
id: 14-20
title: Snake to camel
chapter: 14
type: lesson
skills: sub-case
par: 58
minutes: 8
vscode: sixteen careful edits, or a case-converting extension
vimrc: set ignorecase
open: config.ts

@brief
This config was generated by a script that got both sides wrong. The
keys on the left should be camelCase, as every other object in the
codebase is. The environment variable names on the right should be
upper snake case, as they are in `.env`. Eight lines, sixteen fixes.

The replacement side has case modifiers: `\u` upper-cases the next
character, `\U` upper-cases everything after it until `\E`, and `\l`
and `\L` do the same downwards. Between them, both halves of this
file are two substitutes.

Two warnings. This lesson runs with `ignorecase` on, like most
people's vimrc, so a pattern that says "an underscore and a lower-case
letter" will match an upper-case one too unless you say otherwise.
And the order you do the two halves in matters. Think about why
before you run either.

@keys
| `\u` `\l` | in the replacement: upper- or lower-case the next character |
| `\U` `\L` | upper- or lower-case everything that follows, until `\E` |
| `\E` or `\e` | end the effect of `\U` or `\L` |
| `\C` | in the pattern: case-sensitive, whatever `ignorecase` says |

@hints
Do the right-hand side first. Once the env names are upper case, a
pattern for "underscore then a lower-case letter" cannot touch them —
provided the pattern really is case-sensitive.
--
`:%s/\venv\.\zs\w+/\U&/` shouts every env name. Then
`_([a-z])` with `\u\1` as the replacement camels the keys — but with
`ignorecase` on, `[a-z]` also matches `A-Z`. Put `\C` in the pattern.
--
`:%s/\venv\.\zs\w+/\U&/` then `:%s/\v\C_([a-z])/\u\1/g`. Eight lines,
two commands, and the `\C` is what keeps the second one off the
right-hand side.

@solution
    :%s/\venv\.\zs\w+/\U&/<Enter>  :%s/\v\C_([a-z])/\u\1/g<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Fifty-one keystrokes, and no letter was retyped.

The case modifiers apply to whatever follows them in the replacement,
including `&` and backreferences, which is the point: `\U&` is "the
match, shouted", `\u\1` is "group one with a capital first letter".
`\U` and `\L` run to the end of the replacement unless you stop them
with `\E`, so `\U\1\E_\2` upper-cases only the first group. `\u` and
`\l` affect one character and need no closing.

The order of operations was the judgement in this lesson. Camel the
keys first and the env names go with them; then shouting the env
names turns `databaseUrl` into `DATABASEURL`, and the underscore is
gone for good. Fix the right-hand side first and it is out of reach
of the second command, as long as the second command is
case-sensitive. That `\C` cost two keystrokes and is the difference
between a correct file and one that looks correct until the app fails
to boot. The `n` flag from earlier in the chapter would have told you:
`:%s/\v_([a-z])//gn` reports sixteen matches with `ignorecase` on, and
you were expecting eight.

@start config.ts
import { env } from './env';

export const config = {
  database_url: env.database_url,
  database_pool_size: Number(env.database_pool_size),
  rabbit_url: env.rabbit_url,
  rabbit_prefetch_count: Number(env.rabbit_prefetch_count),
  payment_gateway_base_url: env.payment_gateway_base_url,
  payment_gateway_timeout_ms: Number(env.payment_gateway_timeout_ms),
  email_from_address: env.email_from_address,
  refund_window_days: Number(env.refund_window_days),
};

@expected config.ts
import { env } from './env';

export const config = {
  databaseUrl: env.DATABASE_URL,
  databasePoolSize: Number(env.DATABASE_POOL_SIZE),
  rabbitUrl: env.RABBIT_URL,
  rabbitPrefetchCount: Number(env.RABBIT_PREFETCH_COUNT),
  paymentGatewayBaseUrl: env.PAYMENT_GATEWAY_BASE_URL,
  paymentGatewayTimeoutMs: Number(env.PAYMENT_GATEWAY_TIMEOUT_MS),
  emailFromAddress: env.EMAIL_FROM_ADDRESS,
  refundWindowDays: Number(env.REFUND_WINDOW_DAYS),
};
